color":394,"icon":857},"i-lucide-lightbulb","For in-Filament rendering you almost never call these directly — the infolist component, relation manager, and action all mount the Livewire timeline component, which calls ",[121,860,861],{},"paginate()"," for you. Direct calls are useful for tests, queue workers, exports, and other non-UI consumers.",[133,864,866,867],{"id":865},"trait-helper-paginatetimeline","Trait helper: ",[121,868,869],{},"paginateTimeline()",[117,871,872,873,876,877,880,881,884,885,888],{},"The ",[121,874,875],{},"Relaticle\\ActivityLog\\Concerns\\InteractsWithTimeline"," trait exposes ",[121,878,879],{},"paginateTimeline(?int $perPage = null, int $page = 1)"," as a pass-through to ",[121,882,883],{},"$this->timeline()->paginate(...)",". It's required by the ",[121,886,887],{},"Relaticle\\ActivityLog\\Contracts\\HasTimeline"," interface, so consumers can paginate without going through the builder explicitly:",[141,890,892],{"className":143,"code":891,"language":145,"meta":146,"style":146},"$page = $opportunity->paginateTimeline(perPage: 20, page: 1);\n",[121,893,894],{"__ignoreMap":146},[150,895,896,898,900,902,904,907,909,912,914,916,918,921,923,925,927,930],{"class":152,"line":153},[150,897,157],{"class":156},[150,899,779],{"class":160},[150,901,748],{"class":156},[150,903,751],{"class":156},[150,905,906],{"class":160},"opportunity",[150,908,164],{"class":156},[150,910,911],{"class":167},"paginateTimeline",[150,913,183],{"class":156},[150,915,804],{"class":803},[150,917,548],{"class":156},[150,919,920],{"class":809}," 20",[150,922,263],{"class":156},[150,924,815],{"class":803},[150,926,548],{"class":156},[150,928,929],{"class":809}," 1",[150,931,639],{"class":156},[117,933,934,935,938,939,941],{},"This is the entry point the Livewire timeline component uses internally. Override ",[121,936,937],{},"timeline()"," on the model to customize source registration once and have every caller — including ",[121,940,869],{}," — pick it up.",[133,943,945],{"id":944},"pagination-buffer-mechanics","Pagination buffer mechanics",[117,947,948,949,952,953,956],{},"Pagination uses an over-fetch strategy controlled by the ",[121,950,951],{},"pagination_buffer"," config key (default ",[121,954,955],{},"2","):",[141,958,963],{"className":959,"code":961,"language":962,"meta":146},[960],"language-text","cap = perPage × (page + buffer)\n","text",[121,964,961],{"__ignoreMap":146},[117,966,967,968,971,972,975],{},"Each source is asked for at most ",[121,969,970],{},"cap"," entries via ",[121,973,974],{},"Window::cap",". After dedup and post-source filtering drop entries, the remaining set is sliced down to the requested page. Over-fetching keeps deeper pages from coming up short when entries are removed during the pipeline.",[117,977,978],{},[397,979,980],{},"Trade-off:",[982,983,984,992],"ul",{},[985,986,987,988,991],"li",{},"Higher ",[121,989,990],{},"buffer"," — more memory, more rows scanned per source, but exact pagination at deep pages even when sources collide heavily.",[985,993,994,995,997,998,1000],{},"Lower ",[121,996,990],{}," — less memory, fewer rows scanned, but risk of a deep page returning fewer than ",[121,999,804],{}," entries when dedup removes a lot.",[117,1002,1003,1006,1007,1010,1011,1013],{},[397,1004,1005],{},"When to lower it:"," sources rarely collide (no overlapping ",[121,1008,1009],{},"dedupKey","s) and your filters are permissive. The default ",[121,1012,955],{}," is overkill for single-source timelines.",[117,1015,1016,1019],{},[397,1017,1018],{},"When to raise it:"," sources overlap heavily — for example, a custom source that re-emits events also covered by spatie activity log — and you need exact page sizes at high page numbers.",[117,1021,1022,1023,1025],{},"See ",[126,1024,63],{"href":63}," for the config key.",[1027,1028,1029],"style",{},"html pre.shiki code .sMK4o, html code.shiki .sMK4o{--shiki-light:#39ADB5;--shiki-default:#89DDFF;--shiki-dark:#89DDFF}html pre.shiki code .sTEyZ, html code.shiki